    "q-104215": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104215",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220175/assets/aa9c640445da2c47bfabe3afe2cacb2acd2e67e42e052260c1cdf87d5c81dd16.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?",
      "worked_solution": "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a plane parallel to the $xy$-plane.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since the $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $2,$ we must have $z=2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $z=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
