    "q-104208": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104208",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220175/assets/2499715807dedd0234363454dea33afb592d497005c4f643b03145b6c89cc222.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in cylindrical polar coordinates?",
      "worked_solution": "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $2$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
