    "q-104223": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104223",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "What surface is defined by the equation $z=3$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?",
      "worked_solution": "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nz=3\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that $z$-coordinate of any point on our surface equals $3,$ while $r$ and $\\theta $ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the plane that is parallel to the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220175/assets/ab98ba68465b36fcc3c7ac2de4f50a8f3f16f5e7e10f2c08625cbd933c59f3c4.png)",
