    "q-104219": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104219",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "What surface is defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?",
      "worked_solution": "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220175/assets/0118d67477cd0665d4ae5cbebbf3c3d6f3a6a24f4944977d4301cbaf99d3ecaa.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
