    "q-104224": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104224",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "What surface is defined by the equation $r=6$ in cylindrical polar coordinates?",
      "worked_solution": "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\nr=6\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that the distance from any point on the surface to the $z$-axis equals $6,$ while $\\theta $ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, our surface is the circular cylinder shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220175/assets/66a12bea76428d4cdfff54210546dfea8b0124c1dff1b4986d7bb6b814f34a2f.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-104224-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis",
              "html": "A half-plane that passes through the <mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"126\" style=\"font-size: 119.6%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D467 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>-axis"
            },
            {
